    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Proteasome inhibitors (PIs) are a class of chemotherapeutic drug in treating multiple myeloma (MM), a cancer of plasma cells. PIs are an integral part of MM therapy, but their usage is limited by its cardiovascular side effects. Clinical cases show that patients have suffered from complications such as heart failure and sudden cardiac death after taking these drugs. The mechanism through which PIs cause these effects remains unknown. This study aims to use an iPSC-CM model to recapitulate and investigate the mechanism of cardiotoxicity caused by PIs. First, human iPSC-CMs were treated with three types of PIs (carfilzomib, ixazomib, and bortezomib) at doses and schedules relevant to their clinical usages. Doxorubicin, a well characterized chemotherapeutic known to have cardiac adverse side effects, was used as a positive control. We then conducted a series of in vitro investigations including contractility and viability assays to 1) determine whether iPSC-CMs adequately model clinical features of PI-induced cardiotoxicities and 2) to better understand their effects on the contraction of cardiomyocytes. Results show that the mechanism through which cardiotoxicity occurs is linked to the cells’ inability to contract when treated with moderate to high concentration of the drugs. Cells treated with carfilzomib, bortezomib, and ixazomib respectively showed high, moderate, and low levels of cardiotoxicity. The iPSC-CM model used in this study serves as a high-throughput, efficient approach to screen drugs associated with deaths due to cardiac adverse events in MM patients and will enable future studies to further investigate the effects of PIs on cardiomyocytes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
